{:tier :hand, :verse 1, :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "The source ref-tag read Proverbs 8:28; the verse quoted ('HaShem acquired me as the beginning of His way') is Proverbs 8:22 — normalized to the verse quoted."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:30:00Z", :translated-from :he, :model "claude-opus-4-8 (hand-rendered)", :comments ["<b>In the beginning Elohim created</b> — Rabbi Yitzchak said: The Torah need not have begun except from 'This month shall be for you' (Exodus 12:2), which is the first commandment that Israel was commanded. And what is the reason it opened with 'In the beginning'? So that if the nations of the world should say to Israel, 'You are robbers, for you have conquered for yourselves the lands of seven nations,' they may say to them: 'All the earth belongs to the Holy One, blessed be He; He created it and gave it to whomever was upright in His eyes; by His will He gave it to them, and by His will He took it from them and gave it to us.' This is an aggadah in the language in which our teacher Solomon [Rashi] wrote it in his commentary. And there is [reason] to question it, for it is a great necessity to begin the Torah with 'In the beginning Elohim created,' since this is the root of faith; and one who does not believe this and thinks the world is eternal denies the essential principle and has no Torah at all. And the answer is: because the work of Creation is a deep secret, not understood from the verses, and it cannot be known in its fullness except through the tradition [Kabbalah] going back to Moses our teacher from the mouth of the Gevurah [the Divine Power], and those who know it are obligated to conceal it. Therefore Rabbi Yitzchak said that the beginning of the Torah had no need of 'In the beginning created,' nor the account of what was created on the first day and what was made on the second day and the rest of the days, nor the length concerning the formation of Adam and Eve, their sin and their punishment, and the account of the Garden of Eden and the expulsion of Adam from it — for all this cannot be understood in complete understanding from the writings. And all the more so the account of the generation of the Flood and the Dispersion, in which the need is not great. It would suffice for the people of the Torah without these writings, and they would believe in general in what is mentioned to them in the Ten Commandments (Exodus 20:11): 'For in six days HaShem made the heavens and the earth, the sea, and all that is in them, and rested on the seventh day,' and the [full] knowledge would remain for the select ones among them as a law [given] to Moses from Sinai, together with the Oral Torah. And Rabbi Yitzchak gave a reason for this: that the Torah began with 'In the beginning Elohim created' and the account of the whole matter of formation up to the creation of Adam, and that He gave him dominion over the works of His hands and set all under his feet, and the Garden of Eden — the choicest of the places created in this world — was made a dwelling for his habitation, until his sin drove him out from there. And the people of the generation of the Flood, by their sin, were driven from the whole world, and the righteous one among them alone escaped, he and his sons. And their seed — their sin caused them to be scattered in [various] places and dispersed in [various] lands, and they seized places for themselves according to their families among their nations as it chanced for them. If so, it is fitting that when a nation continues to sin, it should be lost from its place and another nation come to inherit its land — for so has been the judgment of Elohim upon the earth from of old. And all the more so with what is recounted in Scripture, that Canaan was cursed and sold as an eternal slave, and it is not fitting that he inherit the choicest places of settlement; rather the servants of HaShem, the seed of His beloved, shall inherit it, as in the matter that is written (Psalms 105:44): 'And He gave them the lands of nations, and they inherited the labor of peoples, that they might keep His statutes and observe His laws.' That is to say, He drove out from there His rebels and settled in it His worshippers, that they might know that by His service they would inherit it, and if they sinned against Him — the land would vomit them out, as it vomited out the nation that was before them. And that which clarifies the interpretation I have written is their language in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah, section 1), where they said in this language: Rabbi Yehoshua of Sikhnin in the name of Rabbi Levi opened, 'The power of His works He declared to His people' (Psalms 111:6). What is the reason the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed to Israel what was created on the first day and what was created on the second day? Because of the seven nations, that they should not taunt Israel and say to them: Are you not a nation of plunderers? And Israel answers them: And you — is it not plunder in your hands? Are not 'the Caphtorim who came out of Caphtor destroyed them and dwelt in their place' (Deuteronomy 2:23)? The world and its fullness belongs to the Holy One, blessed be He; when He wished, He gave it to you; when He wished, He took it from you and gave it to us. This is what is written (Psalms 111:6), 'to give them the inheritance of nations' — 'the power of His works He declared to His people': in order to give them the inheritance of nations, He declared to them [the account of] 'In the beginning.' And already the matter I have mentioned has come to them from another place. Concerning the mysteries of the work of Creation, our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'The power of His works He declared to His people' — to declare the power of the work of Creation to flesh and blood is impossible; therefore Scripture concealed it for you [with] 'In the beginning Elohim created.' If so, what we have said in this matter is explained.", "<b>In the beginning Elohim created</b> — Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 1:1): This verse says nothing other than 'Expound me,' as our Rabbis expounded it: for the sake of the Torah, which is called 'the beginning [reshit],' as it is said (Proverbs 8:22), 'HaShem acquired me as the beginning of His way,' and for the sake of Israel, who are called 'the beginning,' as it is said (Jeremiah 2:3), 'Israel is holy to HaShem, the beginning of His produce.' And this midrash of our Rabbis is very closed and sealed, for they found many things that are called 'beginning,' and for all of them they have midrashim, and those of little faith would recount most of them. They said (Genesis Rabbah, section 1): By the merit of three things the world was created — by the merit of challah, by the merit of tithes, and by the merit of first-fruits. 'In the beginning Elohim created' — 'beginning' is nothing but challah, as it is said (Numbers 15:20): 'the first [reshit] of your dough.' And 'beginning' is nothing but tithes, as it is said (Deuteronomy 18:4): 'the first [reshit] of your grain.' And 'beginning' is nothing but first-fruits, as it is said (Exodus 23:19): 'the first [reshit] of the first-fruits of your land.' And again they said (ibid.): by the merit of Moses, as it is said (Deuteronomy 33:21): 'And he saw the first [reshit] for himself.' And this their intention is that the word Bereshit hints that the world was created by ten sefirot, and it hints at the sefirah called Chochmah [Wisdom], in which is the foundation of all, as in the matter that is said (Proverbs 3:19): 'HaShem founded the earth with Wisdom' — it is the terumah [heave-offering]. And it is holy, having no measure, on account of the smallness of the created beings' contemplation of it. And when a person counts ten measures and separates one of ten — a hint of the ten sefirot — the wise contemplate the tenth and speak of it. And the challah — a single commandment in the dough — hints at this. And Israel, who are called 'beginning,' is the Assembly of Israel [Knesset Yisrael], likened in the Song of Songs to a bride, whom Scripture called 'daughter' and 'sister' and 'mother,' and this has already come to them in the midrash (Song of Songs Rabbah, section 3): 'with the crown with which his mother crowned him' (Song of Songs 3:11), and in many places. And likewise 'and he saw the first for himself' concerning Moses — they hold that Moses our teacher gazed into the illuminating speculum and saw the first for himself, and therefore merited the Torah. All is one intention for them. And it is impossible to expand upon the interpretation of this matter in writing, and the hint is greatly harmful, for they would form conjectures about it in which there is no truth. But I have mentioned this to stop the mouth of those of little faith and scant wisdom, who scoff at the words of our Rabbis.", "<b>In the beginning</b> — Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 1:1): And if you come to interpret it according to its plain sense, interpret it thus: At the beginning of the creation of the heavens and the earth, when the earth was formlessness and void and darkness, the Holy One, blessed be He, said, 'Let there be light.' If so, all is drawn to the creation of the light. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] interpreted according to this same matter, but he corrected [it]: that the vav in the word 'and the earth' does not serve [as a conjunction], and there are many such in the Scriptures. And the meaning is that at the beginning of the creation of the firmament and the dry land, there was no settlement on the earth, but it was formlessness and void covered with water, and Elohim said, 'Let there be light.' And according to his opinion, nothing was created on the first day but the light. And the difficulty for our teacher Solomon [Rashi] with this interpretation is that he said: if [Scripture] comes to teach the order of the creation with these [words], to say that they preceded — it should have written 'at the first [barishonah],' for there is no 'reshit' in Scripture that is not in the construct state. And behold, 'declaring the end from the beginning [me-reshit]' (Isaiah 46:10). And if he should join it to 'a thing [davar],' this too you may join. And further, 'and he saw the first [reshit] for himself' (Deuteronomy 33:21). And he raised further objections concerning this. And now hear the interpretation of the verse according to its plain sense, correct and clear. The Holy One, blessed be He, created all the created beings from absolute nothingness. And we have in the Holy Tongue no [term] for the bringing forth of being from nothing except the term 'bara' [created]; and nothing that is made — under the sun or above — comes into being from nothing as its first beginning. But He brought forth from utter, absolute nought a very fine element, having no substance, but it is a power that brings into being, ready to receive form and to go forth from potentiality into actuality. And it is the prime matter, called by the Greeks 'hiyuli' [hyle]. And after the hiyuli He created nothing, but formed and made, for from it He brought forth everything and clothed the forms and set them in order. And know that the heavens and all that is in them are one matter, and the earth and all that is in it are one matter. And the Holy One, blessed be He, created these two from nothing, and these two alone were created, and everything is made from them. And this matter, which they called hiyuli, is called in the Holy Tongue 'tohu,' and the word is derived from their language (Kiddushin 40b): 'reconsidering [tohei] the former things.' Because if a person comes to fix a name for it, he is bewildered [tohei] and changes his mind, to call it by another name, for it has not put on a form by which a name could be grasped for it at all. And the form clothed upon this matter is called in the Holy Tongue 'bohu,' and the word is compound, as if to say 'bo hu' [in it is it], like the word 'you cannot do it [asohu]' (Exodus 18:18), which lacks the vav and the alef, [being] 'aso hu.' And this is what Scripture said (Isaiah 34:11): 'And He shall stretch over it the line of tohu and the stones of bohu,' for it is the line by which the craftsman delimits the design of his building and what he hopes to make — derived from 'hope [kaveh] to HaShem' (Psalms 27:14) — and the stones are the forms in the building. And likewise it is written: 'from nought and tohu they are accounted to Him' (Isaiah 40:17), for the tohu is after the nought, and it is not a [substantial] thing. And so they said in the Book of Yetzirah: 'He formed something actual from tohu and made what is not into what is.' And further they said in the Midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah (Sefer HaBahir): Rabbi Berekhiah said, what is the meaning of what is written, 'And the earth was [hayetah] tohu and bohu'? What does 'was' imply? That it had already been tohu. And what is 'bohu'? Rather, it was tohu, and what is 'tohu'? A thing that bewilders [mat'heh] the sons of man, and it returned to bohu. And what is 'bohu'? A thing that has substance in it, as it is written 'bo hu' [in it is it].", "And He said <b>Elohim</b> — the Master of all the powers, for the essence of the word is 'El,' which is [a term for] power, and it is a compound word, 'El Hem' [El — them], as if 'El' is in the construct and 'Hem' [them] hints at all the rest of the powers; that is to say, the power of all the powers. And further a secret will be explained in this. If so, the plain sense of the verses will be correct: its meaning is, at the beginning 'Elohim created the heavens [et ha-shamayim],' for He brought forth their matter from nothing, 'and the earth [ve-et ha-aretz],' for He brought forth its matter from nothing. And 'the earth' includes all four elements, as in 'And the heavens and the earth and all their host were completed' (Genesis 2:1), which includes the entire lower sphere. And likewise 'Praise HaShem from the earth, sea-monsters and all deeps' (Psalms 148:7), and many others besides them. And behold, in this creation, which is like a small fine point having no substance, all the created beings in the heavens and the earth were created.<br>And the word <b>et</b> is like the essence of the thing. And they expounded concerning it that it always [comes] to include, for it is derived from 'morning comes [atah] and also night' (Isaiah 21:12). And so our Rabbis said (Genesis Rabbah, section 1): 'et ha-shamayim,' to include the sun and moon, stars and constellations; 've-et ha-aretz,' to include the trees and grasses and the Garden of Eden. And these comprise all the created beings possessing a body. And after [Scripture] said that at the beginning — in a single utterance — Elohim created the heavens and the earth and all their host, it returned and explained that the earth after this creation was 'tohu,' that is to say, matter having no substance; and it was 'bohu,' for He clothed it in form. And it explained that in this form is the form of the four elements, which are the fire and the water and the earth[-element] and the air, and the word 'the earth' includes these four. And the fire is called 'darkness [choshekh],' because the elemental fire is dark; for were it red, it would redden the night for us. And the water in which the earth[-dust] is mixed is called 'tehom [deep],' and therefore the waters of the sea are called 'tehomot [deeps],' as it is written (Exodus 15:5): 'the deeps cover them'; 'the deeps congealed' (ibid. v. 8); 'the deep surrounds me' (Jonah 2:6). And the floor of the sea is called 'tehom': 'And He rebuked the Sea of Reeds and it dried up, and He led them through the deeps as through the wilderness' (Psalms 106:9); 'who led them through the deeps like a horse in the wilderness' (Isaiah 63:13). And the air is called 'ruach [wind/spirit].' And it is already known that the four elements are a single mass, and its pillar is the sphere of the earth. And the water surrounds the earth, and the air surrounds the water, and the fire surrounds the air. And Scripture said that the earth put on form, and the fire was surrounding above upon the water and the earth[-dust] mingled together, and the wind blows and enters into the darkness and hovers over the water. And it appears to me that this point, when it put on the form and became bohu, is what the Sages call the 'Foundation Stone [even shetiyah]' (Yoma 54b), from which the world was founded [nishtat]. And behold, the measure of the verses is: at the beginning Elohim created from nothing the heavens, and created from nothing the earth, and the earth when it was created was tohu and was bohu, and in them were darkness and water and earth[-dust], and a wind blowing upon the water. And behold, all was created and made. And [Scripture] joined 'the wind' to 'Elohim,' because it is finer than all of them, and above them, only that it hovers over the face of the water by the utterance of the Holy One, blessed be He. And if you seek the creation of the angels, who are not body, this is not explained in the Torah. And they expounded concerning them that they were created on the second day, so that you should not say that they assisted in the creation of the world. But if you merit and understand the secret of the word Bereshit, and why [Scripture] did not first say 'Elohim created in the beginning,' you will know that by way of truth Scripture tells of the lower [worlds] and hints at the upper [worlds]. And the word Bereshit hints at the Chochmah [Wisdom], which is the beginning of beginnings, as I have mentioned. And therefore they rendered [it] in the Jerusalem Targum 'be-chokhmeta [with Wisdom],' and the word is crowned with the crown [kether] of the letter bet."]}
